@page "/support"

<PageTitle>Support | It's About Bloody Time</PageTitle>

<section class="event-page information-page support-page">
    <div class="page-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Help with your archive</p>
        <h1>Support</h1>
        <p class="page-introduction">A quick guide to creating and caring for the timelines and events in your archive.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="information-content support-guide">
        <section class="support-section">
            <p class="support-section-label">Timelines</p>
            <h2>Create and manage a timeline</h2>
            <ol class="support-steps">
                <li>
                    <strong>Start with a clear subject.</strong>
                    From the Home page, select <strong>Create timeline</strong>. Give the timeline a specific title, such as “The Space Race” or “My Family History”, then add a description that explains what visitors will find in it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Add an optional cover image.</strong>
                    A cover image helps distinguish the timeline on the Home page and appears in the editor. Choose a JPEG, PNG, GIF, or WebP image that is 5 MB or smaller.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Update the details later.</strong>
                    After creating a timeline, you are taken directly to its editor. Select <strong>Edit Details</strong> whenever you need to change the title, description, or cover image. Existing events are not affected.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Delete it carefully.</strong>
                    Select <strong>Delete Timeline</strong> in the editor and confirm the prompt using the timeline name. This permanently removes the timeline, every event in it, and their images, so use it only when you no longer need the complete record.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="support-section">
            <p class="support-section-label">Events</p>
            <h2>Add and update events</h2>
            <ol class="support-steps">
                <li>
                    <strong>Add an event.</strong>
                    In the timeline editor, select <strong>Add event</strong>. Add a concise title, a short summary for quick scanning, and a fuller description for the detail view. Attach an optional image to make the event easier to recognise.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Set the date range.</strong>
                    Choose a start date and end date for every event. Use the same date for one-day events, or use a longer range for periods such as a war, reign, expedition, or building project. The end date cannot be earlier than the start date.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Review an event.</strong>
                    Select a row in the event table to open its preview on the right. The preview shows the image, date range, title, summary, and full description together, so you can check the entry before changing it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Edit or remove an event.</strong>
                    Use <strong>Edit</strong> in the preview to change any event detail, including its dates and image. Use <strong>Delete</strong> to remove the event after confirming its name. Select <strong>Close</strong> when you are finished reviewing an event to return to the full-width table.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="support-section">
            <p class="support-section-label">Explore</p>
            <h2>Find and view your history</h2>
            <div class="support-tips">
                <p><strong>Find a timeline:</strong> Use the search field on the Home page to filter timelines by title or description as you type. Select the <strong>×</strong> in the field to clear the search and show every timeline again.</p>
                <p><strong>Render a timeline:</strong> Select <strong>Render Timeline</strong> in the editor to arrange events along a date-based visual timeline. Events that span more than one day are displayed as ranges, and each card can be selected to read its full details.</p>
                <p><strong>Explore longer histories:</strong> The rendered timeline has its own horizontal scrollbar at the top. Drag it to travel through lengthy timelines without losing the date range labels.</p>
                <p><strong>Keep entries useful:</strong> Write a short summary for quick scanning, then use the full description to preserve the detail and context worth returning to. Consistent titles and date ranges make the visual timeline easier to understand.</p>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
</section>
